{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/curseborne/1545983-weapon-and-armor-tags","id":1545983,"title":"Weapon and Armor Tags","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Curseborne"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-11-15T19:42:20-08:00","id":1545983,"title":"Weapon and Armor Tags","content":"New poster / occasional lurker here with a question - I noticed that the Curseborne manuscript provides equipment tags, but no list of examples of common equipment using those tags. For those who backed that project, was there a list of examples using the same (or a similar set of) tags in the Storypath Ultra Core Manual? Or are Storyguides expected to create their own equipment tables / hash each item out individually with players? (I'll admit, I kinda went overboard drafting armor and weapon lists myself already - nothing huge, but there are only so many combinations of tags that make sense, after all!)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","name":"r0gue_rob0t","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-16T05:07:49-08:00","id":1545993,"title":"","content":"I believe you're just meant to make your own, everything gets exactly two tags so it's fairly simple and as you say there's only so many options that make sense.<br /> <br /> [Edit]<br /> <br /> To clarify, I don't think you're meant to make up a list, I think it's meant to be ad hoc. Like when a weapon shows up, you assign it whatever tags seem appropriate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28625,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28625-nobody-special","user":"Nobody Special","date":". 11-16-2024, 12:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28625-nobody-special","name":"Nobody Special","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"219"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-17T07:55:17-08:00","id":1546029,"title":"","content":"I thought that might be the case...<br /> <br /> Well, then, I feel I would be remiss not to share what I did come up with, for anyone to use (or modify) freely. All ideas provided below are intentionally limited to two tags - I'll get to the why in a moment. First up:<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>Armor</b></span><br /> <br /> <b>SPORTS GEAR - &lt; No tags &gt;</b><br /> <i>Padded protective gear for high-impact sports.</i><br /> Dark Eras: cloth and leather gambesons<br /> <br /> <b>WORK CLOTHES - Concealable</b><br /> <i>Heavy, protective clothing for jobsite safety.</i><br /> Also used for: leather coats and jackets<br /> Dark Eras: work clothes<br /> <br /> <b>HAZMAT SUIT - Exquisite Material</b><br /> <i>A sealed suit to protect against nuclear, biological, or chemical hazards.</i><br /> <br /> <b>BALLISTIC JACKET - Padded</b><br /> <i>A basic military jacket composed of bullet-resistant fibers.</i><br /> Dark Eras: bone armors, boiled leather scale and lamellar armors<br /> <br /> <b>BALLISTIC VEST - Concealable + Padded</b><br /> <i>A low-profile bullet-resistant fiber vest wearable under other clothes.</i><br /> Dark Eras: chain shirts<br /> <br /> <b>BUNKER GEAR - Exquisite Material + Padded</b><br /> <i>A firefighter's suit, made to protect from heat and falling debris alike.</i><br /> <br /> <b>RIOT GEAR - Padded (x2)</b><br /> <i>A heavily-padded suit for riot control, resistant to repeated strikes.</i><br /> Dark Eras: chain hauberks, brigandine, coat-of-plates, metal scale and lamellar armors<br /> <br /> <b>BALLISTIC PLATE - Impenetrable + Padded</b><br /> <i>A ballistic jacket with added bullet-proof ceramic plate inserts.</i><br /> Dark Eras: metal banded and plate armors<br /> <br /> <b>Updates:</b> I moved around a couple &quot;Dark Eras&quot; items (mostly just the full-body chain armor and its equivalents being separated from the boiled leather and bone armors to match &quot;riot gear&quot;). The tag choices generally still follow the same logic here: <i>Impenetrable</i> for &quot;hard&quot; or bulletproof armors, <i>Padded</i> applied once for any actual armor as opposed to protective clothing and twice for the heavier but non-bulletproof items, <i>Concealable</i> if it is or can be worn under normal outerwear, and <i>Exquisite Material</i> for a couple items of modern protective wear designed against NBC or fire hazards.<br /> <br /> <b>Old Notes:</b> Looking at the Blessed Plate Relic, I noticed right away that the game seems to want mundane gear limited to two tags. The Relic provides Elemental Resistance (Fire) and Exquisite Material as &quot;magical&quot; benefits, and uses Impenetrable and Padded to represent a mundane suit of plate armor underneath the magic. From here, I derived an &quot;upper limit&quot; for mundane equipment, and worked my way down. I default to common modern items for naming purposes on each profile, but will note when alternative items (including pre-modern equipment) use the same Tags.<br /> <br /> To explain my thinking in the tag choices here: I used Impenetrable exclusively for &quot;hard&quot; armors - hard-plated articles that <i>will</i> stop bullets, even &quot;armor piercing&quot; bullets - while the first application of Padded separates &quot;soft&quot; armors from non-armor items which still offer some degree of protection from bodily harm. As for the work clothes and <i>Concealable</i> - the tag itself does specify &quot;or indistinguishable from ordinary clothing.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3388,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","user":"r0gue_rob0t","date":".  Updating the profiles after discussion in thread. 12-04-2024, 08:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","name":"r0gue_rob0t","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-17T08:44:08-08:00","id":1546032,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>Projectile Weapons</b></span><br /> <br /> <b>PISTOL - Concealable</b><br /> <i>A small, easily concealed firearm.</i><br /> Dark Eras: blowguns, slings, pistol crossbows, pre-modern pistols<br /> <br /> <b>SHOTGUN - &lt; Variable tags &gt;</b><br /> <i>A long-barreled firearm capable of accepting a variety of ammunition.</i><br /> Variable tags determined by type of ammunition used:<br /> - Birdshot: <b>Long Range + Wounding</b><br /> - Buckshot, Slugs: <b>Brutal + Wounding</b><br /> - Beanbag, Rubber Shot: <b>Stunning</b><br /> - Rock Salt: <b>Painful</b><br /> - Magnesium (&quot;Dragon's Breath&quot;): <b>Flaming</b><br /> Dark Eras: blunderbusses (buckshot only)<br /> <br /> <b>RIFLE - Long Range + &lt; Variable tag &gt;</b><br /> <i>A long-barreled firearm made for precision shooting.</i><br /> Variable tag determined by type of ammunition used:<br /> - Full Metal Jacket: <b>Piercing</b><br /> - Hollowpoint: <b>Wounding</b><br /> <br /> <b>MACHINE PISTOL - Brutal</b><br /> <i>A pistol-sized automatic firearm.</i><br /> <br /> <b>ASSAULT RIFLE - Brutal + &lt; Variable tag &gt;</b><br /> <i>A long-barreled firearm made for automatic fire.</i><br /> Variable tag determined by type of ammunition used:<br /> - Full Metal Jacket: <b>Piercing</b><br /> - Hollowpoint: <b>Wounding</b><br /> <br /> <b>MACHINE GUN - Heavy Weapon</b><br /> <i>A large, normally crew-served weapon, too heavy to wield in hand for all but the strongest.</i><br /> <br /> <b>FLAMETHROWER - Flaming</b><br /> <i>A weapon that sprays a line of burning fuel.</i><br /> <br /> <b>TASER - Painful + Stunning</b><br /> <i>A modified pistol that fires darts electrified by trailing wires.</i><br /> <br /> <b>CROSSBOW: Piercing + Wounding</b><br /> <i>A bow with added mechanisms to draw, hold, and release the string.</i><br /> Dark Eras: muskets (unless modern armors are available)<br /> <br /> <b>BOW - Wounding</b><br /> <i>A muscle-powered weapon using an elastic string to launch arrows.</i><br /> Also used for: sawn-off shotguns (loaded with birdshot, buckshot, or slugs; other ammunition uses the same tags as in a normal shotgun)<br /> Dark Eras: muskets (if modern armors are available), dragons (pistol blunderbusses)<br /> <br /> <b>Updates:</b> Many more changes here. <i>Concealable</i> is now restricted to weapons that can be *easily* hidden under clothing (in short, not a shotgun, even a sawn-off). <i>Long Range</i> is exclusively a property of precision-shooting rifles (ignoring arcing fire from bows). <i>Stunning</i> is now reserved for &quot;less-lethal&quot; weapons designed to briefly immobilize a target through &quot;cushioned&quot; impact or electric shock, while <i>Painful</i> is added for &quot;less-lethal&quot; weapons that produce a lasting &quot;stinging&quot; effect. <i>Piercing</i> is restricted to weapons which can *effectively* penetrate ballistic cloth (with muskets picking it up in Dark Eras where ballistic cloth isn't a thing) - this does leave no distinction in tags between pistols and pre-modern weapons like slings, but that and the issue with muskets are why I chose to move most pre-modern items to the newly-added &quot;Dark Eras&quot; notes.<br /> <br /> Other tags retain the same general logic as before: <i>Heavy Weapon</i> for weapons that begin to exceed personal scale (though it could also be used for fragmentation grenades to make purchasing Tricks for extra targets easier), <i>Wounding</i> for weapons with large or expanding projectiles designed for significant tissue damage, <i>Brutal</i> for personal-scale automatics (and buckshot), <i>Flaming</i> for incendiary weapons.<br /> <br /> <b>Old Notes:</b> Expanding from what I inferred for armor, I divided weapons into three general categories - blunt, edged, and projectile - centered around three tags - <i>Stunning</i>, <i>Wounding</i>, and <i>Piercing</i>, respectively. Not all weapons in each category carry the associated tag (knives, for example, lack Wounding to differentiate them from one-handed swords), and there is some crossover between categories (especially with <i>Piercing</i> and <i>Wounding</i>). Blunt and edged weapons use Close Combat (though do include thrown weapons with the <i>Versatile</i> tag), while projectile weapons use Ranged Combat. <i>Brutal</i> is reserved for especially large or hard-hitting Close Combat weapons, or for shotguns and automatics among Ranged Combat weapons.<br /> <br /> (I deliberately reserved <i>Heavy Weapon</i> exclusively for the machine gun, making it the only personal weapon listed to have Power Advantage. Everything else is absolutely intended to run into trouble against the Durability Advantage of medieval plate and modern ballistic jackets with ceramic plate inserts - not even a chainsaw gets <i>Heavy Weapon</i>, as will be seen later. For the combat-focused Accursed: learn Brutality. Use Brutality. Love Brutality. It's available to everyone at Entanglement 3.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3388,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","user":"r0gue_rob0t","date":".  Updating the profiles after discussion in thread. 12-04-2024, 10:35 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","name":"r0gue_rob0t","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-17T11:00:00-08:00","id":1546036,"title":"","content":"<b><span style=\"font-size:16px\">Edged Weapons</span></b><br /> <br /> <b>SHIV - Concealable</b><br /> <i>Any small, sharp object or sharpened piece of debris.</i><br /> Examples: balisong, butcher's knife, flint knife, kitchen knife, pocket knife, prison shank, razor, scalpel<br /> <br /> <b>DAGGER - Concealable + Light Weapon</b><br /> <i>A small blade made to be used as a weapon.</i><br /> Examples: combat knife, hunting knife, kirpan, keris, kukri, tanto<br /> Also used for: push dagger (katar), sai<br /> <br /> <b>THROWING KNIVES - Light Weapon + Versatile</b><br /> <i>A set of several small knives balanced for throwing.</i><br /> Also used for: chakram, shuriken<br /> <br /> <b>SLASHING SWORD - Light Weapon + Wounding</b><br /> <i>A one-handed sword designed for cutting or chopping strikes.</i><br /> Examples: arming sword, cutlass, falchion, jian, khopesh, macuahuitl, saber, scimitar, shotel, spatha, talwar, wakizashi<br /> <br /> <b>THRUSTING SWORD - Light Weapon + Piercing</b><br /> <i>A one-handed sword designed to thrust between armor joints.</i><br /> Examples: estoc, rapier, tuck<br /> <br /> <b>LONGSWORD - Brutal + Wounding</b><br /> <i>A massive two-handed (or hand-and-a-half) sword.</i><br /> Examples: claymore, katana, odachi, zhanmadao, zweihander<br /> <br /> <b>SPEAR - Reach + Versatile</b><br /> <i>A long pole with a sharp tip, designed to be wielded in hand or thrown.</i><br /> Examples: assegai, harpoon, javelin, pilum, trident<br /> Also used for: atlatl, woomera<br /> <br /> <b>HALBERD - Reach + Wounding</b><br /> <i>A long spear fitted with an axe head below the tip.</i><br /> Also used for: glaive, horseman's pick, lance, lucerne hammer, naginata, partisan, pike, poleaxe, tepoztopilli, yari<br /> <br /> <b>AXE - Wounding + Wrecking</b><br /> <i>A large, broad-bladed cutting tool, well-suited for use as a weapon.</i><br /> Examples: battle axe, dane axe, fire axe, masakari, parashu, sagaris<br /> Also used for: adze, ice axe, machete, mattock, pickaxe, scythe<br /> <br /> <b>HATCHET - Versatile + Wounding</b><br /> <i>A small axe weighted for throwing.</i><br /> Examples: francisca, tomahawk<br /> <br /> <b>CHAIN WHIP - Light Weapon + Reach</b><br /> <i>A chain of small metal rods connecting a dart to a handle.</i><br /> Also used for: kusarigama, rope dart, urumi<br /> <br /> <b>CHAINSAW - Deadly + Wounding</b><br /> <i>A large tree-felling tool, unwieldy in the hands of all but the strongest.</i><br /> <br /> <b>Updates:</b> Fewer to note here, though axes now get the <i>Wrecking</i> tag to represent their function as tools. &quot;Conventional&quot; whips have been moved to the bludgeons, and the claws / bagh nahk has been relisted as an example alternative use of the knuckleduster profile; the &quot;chain whip&quot; listed here is the wushu (Chinese martial arts) weapon.<br /> <br /> As for the tags themselves - <i>Concealable</i> is used for weapons that can be easily hidden in clothing, as with projectile weapons. <i>Light Weapon</i> is applied to weapons that specifically benefit from finesse (bearing in mind that we currently lack any means outside of magic to apply it to unarmed attacks). <i>Versatile</i> is used for throwing weapons (where the second tag can still be applied normally). <i>Wounding</i> is for weapons that produce large laceration wounds. <i>Piercing</i> is limited to thrusting swords which sacrifice their cutting edges in favor of a design that exploits the joints in hard armors. <i>Brutal</i> is used as both upgrade and replacement for <i>Light Weapon</i> on heavier longswords. <i>Reach</i> is used for polearms and longer lashing weapons. <i>Deadly</i> is still used exclusively for the chainsaw as a dubious &quot;upgrade&quot; over the longsword's <i>Brutal</i>, to make the chainsaw a terrifying &quot;one-hit kill&quot; weapon against unarmored targets, but just a *slightly* more dangerous improvised weapon against anything with any amount of armor (including &quot;armor&quot; provided by spells).<br /> <br /> As an aside, depending on how your Storyguide reads the restrictions on applying the <i>Deadly</i> and <i>Wounding</i> tags with respect to armor, remember that it may be necessary to purchase the Sunder Trick before either can be applied to armored targets, even after their bonus injury boxes from armor have been exhausted. If your table uses that interpretation of these tags, Spells that provide the benefits of physical armor implicitly render the subject almost completely immune to both tags - the Red Death spell can get around this as long as Mental Fortitude is not active, as can Bombardment or Weaponmaster's Skill (by choosing <i>Elemental Attunement</i>) absent some form of armor with <i>Elemental Resistance</i>.<br /> <br /> <b>Old Notes:</b> Throwing knives lose <i>Concealable</i> because of the two tag limit and the assumption that you are hiding more than one of them. Bayonets are listed as a variant of pike assuming they are being mounted on the barrel of a rifle or musket - some are, of course, designed to be separated from the firearm and wielded in hand as a dagger.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3388,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","user":"r0gue_rob0t","date":".  Updating the profiles after discussion in thread. 12-05-2024, 12:00 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","name":"r0gue_rob0t","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-17T12:03:41-08:00","id":1546040,"title":"","content":"<b><span style=\"font-size:16px\">Blunt Weapons</span></b><br /> <br /> <b>CLUB - &lt; No tags &gt;</b><br /> <i>Any small blunt object used as a weapon, from sticks to police batons.</i><br /> Examples: billy club, crowbar, jitte, nightstick, small hammer, sports bat or stick, tonfa, tree branch, walking stick, weighted flashlight<br /> Also used for: most improvised weapons, pistol whip, rifle buttstroke<br /> <br /> <b>STUN BATON - Painful + Stunning</b><br /> <i>A handheld prod with an electrified tip.</i><br /> <br /> <b>SAP - Concealable + Stunning</b><br /> <i>A small baton with a weighted head, made for concealment and king hits.</i><br /> Examples: blackjack, cosh<br /> Also used for: war fans (tessen)<br /> <br /> <b>SLEDGEHAMMER - Brutal + Wrecking</b><br /> <i>A blunt tool with a heavy weighted hammer head.</i><br /> Also used for: mace, maul, morning star, otsuchi, sali, shillelagh, war club, war hammer<br /> <br /> <b>STAFF - Brutal + Reach</b><br /> <i>A light weight but long stick weapon.</i><br /> Examples: bo, gun, quarterstaff<br /> <br /> <b>FLAIL - Brutal + Light Weapon</b><br /> <i>A pair of short rods or a rod and weighted ball connected by a short chain.</i><br /> Examples: nunchaku, three-section staff<br /> <br /> <b>WHIP - Painful + Reach</b><br /> <i>A flexible, stinging cord designed for pain compliance.</i><br /> <br /> <b>BOLAS - Stunning + Versatile</b><br /> <i>Hard, spherical bludgeons connected by a long cord, designed for snaring targets at a distance.</i><br /> <br /> <b>BOOMERANG - Brutal + Versatile</b><br /> <i>A curved club, designed to maintain an even flight path when thrown.</i><br /> Examples: rungu, ula, waddy<br /> <br /> <b>KNUCKLEDUSTER - Concealable + Worn</b><br /> <i>A hard striking surface worn over the fingers.</i><br /> Examples: brass knuckles, weighted-knuckle gloves<br /> Also used for: bagh nakh, gauntlet, kakute, steel-toed boot<br /> <br /> <b>SHIELD - Shield + Stunning</b><br /> <i>A barrier carried in hand or strapped to an arm for intercepting attacks.</i><br /> <br /> <b>METEOR HAMMER - Reach + &lt; Variable tag &gt;</b><br /> <i>A length of chain connecting paired metal striking heads.</i><br /> Variable tag determined by type of attached striking head:<br /> - Solid head: <b>Brutal + Reach</b><br /> - Burning brazier: <b>Flaming + Reach</b><br /> <br /> <b>Updates:</b> <i>Stunning</i> has been removed from a lot of these, and is now restricted to electric / immobilizing weapons (in like fashion to the projectile weapons). <i>Painful</i> has been added for &quot;stinging&quot; weapons, and <i>Wrecking</i> for larger hammer-headed weapons to reflect more destructive uses as tools. <i>Brutal</i> picks up a lot of the slack from the removal of <i>Stunning</i>, and now serves as the tag to emphasize bludgeons made as *deliberate* weapons. Shields are no longer split by size, and use only one profile (as the buckler really couldn't justify having <i>Light Weapon</i>). The meteor hammer now has a variable tag to represent the different heads it might be constructed with.<br /> <br /> <i>Concealable</i>, <i>Light Weapon</i>, <i>Reach</i>, and <i>Versatile</i> follow the same logic as with blades. <i>Shield</i> is for shields. <i>Worn</i> is for articles of clothing as weapons and weapons worn on the hands or feet (while I could see an argument for shields to have it, bucklers *don't* work that way, and larger shields should have Stunning for bashing foes).<br /> <br /> <b>Old Notes:</b> The boomerang lacks <i>Returning</i> on purpose - apply it with magic, or swap the <i>Brutal</i> tag for it if you want combat boomerangs that somehow come back to the thrower. And I know Shaolin meteor hammers were never actually used in historical combat, but with the machine gun, flamethrower, and chainsaw all being a thing, blunt weapons deserve stylish options, too!<br /> <br /> <b>Final Comments:</b> <u><i>A huge thank you to Nobody Special and Sergeant Brother</i></u> for challenging my thinking in making this list and pushing me to defend some of my original choices and reconsider others. You two are amazing, and even if neither of you ever use it, this list would be far worse without your input. This list obviously isn't any kind of &quot;be-all-end-all&quot; on the matter - I'm no subject-matter expert, just another hobbyist like (most) of the rest of us here, and the game directly tells Storyguides to let players make their own choices on tags - but I hope it provides people a useful starting point in choosing tags as a way of both (narratively) framing and (mechanically) informing the differences in what kinds of equipment their characters are carrying.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3388,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","user":"r0gue_rob0t","date":".  Updating the profiles after discussion in thread. 12-05-2024, 12:51 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","name":"r0gue_rob0t","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-19T05:19:08-08:00","id":1546126,"title":"","content":"It's interesting how different your interpretations are from mine. I don't think I'd put stunning on so many things for example, and I think I'd put Long Range on hunting rifles, especially if I was using Versatile for throwing weapons. <br /> <br /> I think it's also interesting that what tags &quot;mean&quot; depends a lot on what you use other tags to mean. For example if you give all firearms Penetrating, it follows that anti-balliatic armour needs Invulnerable.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28625-nobody-special","name":"Nobody Special","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"219"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-19T07:46:10-08:00","id":1546128,"title":"","content":"How combat and Damage works in Storypath is something I'm having some difficulty groking.<br /> <br /> I don't suppose one of you can break it down for me like I'm 5?<br /> <br /> It's strange there's no damage values to the weapons yet we have health levels of varying types. Combat in WoD and Cod could be very deadly, yet I've heard Nobody mention it's near impossible to inflict more than 1 health level of damage in an exchange under the Curseborne system.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3769-orphan81","name":"Orphan81","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3769_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"145"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-19T09:09:04-08:00","id":1546131,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Orphan81</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/curseborne/1545983-weapon-and-armor-tags?p=1546128#post1546128\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How combat and Damage works in Storypath is something I'm having some difficulty groking.<br /> <br /> I don't suppose one of you can break it down for me like I'm 5?<br /> <br /> It's strange there's no damage values to the weapons yet we have health levels of varying types. Combat in WoD and Cod could be very deadly, yet I've heard Nobody mention it's near impossible to inflict more than 1 health level of damage in an exchange under the Curseborne system.</div> </div> </div> </div>Rather than trying to explain it like you're five, it might help if I explain it like you're somebody familiar with WoD/CofD and not with SPU (that's basically where I am so I think you'll wind up on a similar journey to me).<br /> <br /> So first off, context. My experience is that WoD/CofD has a <b>massive</b> Rocket Tag issue. I'm running Vampire the Dark Ages and while the PCs are fairly experienced by now it didn't take us that long to get to the point where the Brujah just deletes anything he wins initiative against. The SPU system seems explicitly designed to avoid that, which I think is good.<br /> <br /> The other bit of context is that this system seems designed to be cinematic: you fight harder as you get more injured, low-level NPCs only have one Hit Point, PCs can take a hell of a pounding before they get Taken Out and they only die if it's dramatically appropriate.<br /> <br /> As for how it actually works, this is definitely an unlearn-everything-you-have-learned moment.<br /> <br /> When you attack somebody you make a Close Combat + Might or Ranged Combat + Dexterity roll against a difficulty their Defence. This is always fixed for NPCs, PCs get to boost their defence for the full round by taking a Defensive Action (this means rolling dice equal to their Stamina). As with all rolls in SPU pretty much all the bonus effects you get come from buying <b>tricks</b>.<br /> <br /> If you successfully attack an opponent, which means if you get enough successes to match their Defence, you deal one damage. This is always the same no matter what you attack with.<br /> <br /> If you want to do extra damage, your only option by <b>default</b> is to buy the <b>Critical</b> Trick, which costs 3 successes (over and above the successes you need to match difficulty). This means you do 2 damage. Some Spells allow you to do more damage than that but they're extremely rare.<br /> <br /> Weapons never just do extra damage. Having a weapon grants an Enhancement on attack actions (this is just part of the general rules for equipment) and each weapon has up to two tags that let it inflict extra Status Effects.<br /> <br /> I think for me what made this make sense was taking it out of the white room and into the context of actual play; that is, it needs to be seen as integrated with the Antagonist rules and the prevalence of the Rocket Tag problem in the other games.<br /> <br /> Adversaries/NPCs in Curseborne all have a Template of either Shiver, Fright, Terror, Nightmare or Shattered Space. This template gives them either 1, 4, 10, or 12 Injuries (shattered spaces and terrors both get 10). Notice that this means:<ul><li>A Shiver goes down to a single hit, nearly always.</li> <li>A Fright goes down in 2-3 hits depending on how effectively you can buy criticals.</li> <li>A Terror or Nightmare takes a long time to wear down and so you'll actually get mileage out of things like Bleeding and Poison. They're almost guaranteed to survive at least one round of combat.</li> </ul>Consistently (and I strongly value consistency in game design), SPU prioritises how something works in play over what it's supposed to represent in the real world. It's not literally the case that a very experienced Venator or Sister of the Cross is somehow extremely resistant to bullets, it's that if the purpose of an experienced Venator or Sister of the Cross is to be a mechanical threat to the party, they need to not be trivially removable with a single gunshot.<br /> <br /> Of course there are massive personal preference issues here. Some players want to feel like an human-sized human-shaped target should always be killable with a single well-placed gunshot because they always want the option to do that Indiana Jones scene where he just shoots the guy. Others prefer there to be built in protection for pacing. Although I'll want to test to be sure I *lean* towards preferring the SPU method because I like that the game has thought about the alpha strike problem for me.<br /> <br /> Hope that helps.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28625-nobody-special","name":"Nobody Special","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"219"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-19T11:44:55-08:00","id":1546144,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nobody Special</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/curseborne/1545983-weapon-and-armor-tags?p=1546131#post1546131\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Rather than trying to explain it like you're five, it might help if I explain it like you're somebody familiar with WoD/CofD and not with SPU (that's basically where I am so I think you'll wind up on a similar journey to me).<br /> <br /> So first off, context. My experience is that WoD/CofD has a <b>massive</b> Rocket Tag issue. I'm running Vampire the Dark Ages and while the PCs are fairly experienced by now it didn't take us that long to get to the point where the Brujah just deletes anything he wins initiative against. The SPU system seems explicitly designed to avoid that, which I think is good.<br /> <br /> The other bit of context is that this system seems designed to be cinematic: you fight harder as you get more injured, low-level NPCs only have one Hit Point, PCs can take a hell of a pounding before they get Taken Out and they only die if it's dramatically appropriate.<br /> <br /> As for how it actually works, this is definitely an unlearn-everything-you-have-learned moment.<br /> <br /> When you attack somebody you make a Close Combat + Might or Ranged Combat + Dexterity roll against a difficulty their Defence. This is always fixed for NPCs, PCs get to boost their defence for the full round by taking a Defensive Action (this means rolling dice equal to their Stamina). As with all rolls in SPU pretty much all the bonus effects you get come from buying <b>tricks</b>.<br /> <br /> If you successfully attack an opponent, which means if you get enough successes to match their Defence, you deal one damage. This is always the same no matter what you attack with.<br /> <br /> If you want to do extra damage, your only option by <b>default</b> is to buy the <b>Critical</b> Trick, which costs 3 successes (over and above the successes you need to match difficulty). This means you do 2 damage. Some Spells allow you to do more damage than that but they're extremely rare.<br /> <br /> Weapons never just do extra damage. Having a weapon grants an Enhancement on attack actions (this is just part of the general rules for equipment) and each weapon has up to two tags that let it inflict extra Status Effects.<br /> <br /> I think for me what made this make sense was taking it out of the white room and into the context of actual play; that is, it needs to be seen as integrated with the Antagonist rules and the prevalence of the Rocket Tag problem in the other games.<br /> <br /> Adversaries/NPCs in Curseborne all have a Template of either Shiver, Fright, Terror, Nightmare or Shattered Space. This template gives them either 1, 4, 10, or 12 Injuries (shattered spaces and terrors both get 10). Notice that this means:<ul><li>A Shiver goes down to a single hit, nearly always.</li> <li>A Fright goes down in 2-3 hits depending on how effectively you can buy criticals.</li> <li>A Terror or Nightmare takes a long time to wear down and so you'll actually get mileage out of things like Bleeding and Poison. They're almost guaranteed to survive at least one round of combat.</li> </ul>Consistently (and I strongly value consistency in game design), SPU prioritises how something works in play over what it's supposed to represent in the real world. It's not literally the case that a very experienced Venator or Sister of the Cross is somehow extremely resistant to bullets, it's that if the purpose of an experienced Venator or Sister of the Cross is to be a mechanical threat to the party, they need to not be trivially removable with a single gunshot.<br /> <br /> Of course there are massive personal preference issues here. Some players want to feel like an human-sized human-shaped target should always be killable with a single well-placed gunshot because they always want the option to do that Indiana Jones scene where he just shoots the guy. Others prefer there to be built in protection for pacing. Although I'll want to test to be sure I *lean* towards preferring the SPU method because I like that the game has thought about the alpha strike problem for me.<br /> <br /> Hope that helps.</div> </div> </div> </div>This helps a lot, thank you. <br /> <br /> I can see how this is great for games like Trinity and Scion for epic, cinematic battles of Superheroes and Gods. <br /> <br /> But I'm not sure how I feel about it for a horror game. I saw the deadliness of WoD and Cod as a feature. There were powers and abilities that made characters more survivable (Fort and Celerity for Vamps and Garou in general were tough as nails) but the default of being squishy seemed to fit for a Supernatural horror world. <br /> <br /> I liken it to Invincible, which is almost horror like in it's presentation. These Supernatural beings are capable of tearing mortals and one another apart quite easily. <br /> <br /> It's not terrible, but it certainly changes the way things feel if every fight that matters is a slug fest and there are 1 hit point &quot;Goons&quot; the PCs are supposed to be able to take in swarms.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3769-orphan81","name":"Orphan81","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3769_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"145"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-19T12:12:40-08:00","id":1546146,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Orphan81</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/curseborne/1545983-weapon-and-armor-tags?p=1546144#post1546144\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This helps a lot, thank you.<br /> <br /> I can see how this is great for games like Trinity and Scion for epic, cinematic battles of Superheroes and Gods.<br /> <br /> But I'm not sure how I feel about it for a horror game. I saw the deadliness of WoD and Cod as a feature. There were powers and abilities that made characters more survivable (Fort and Celerity for Vamps and Garou in general were tough as nails) but the default of being squishy seemed to fit for a Supernatural horror world.<br /> <br /> I liken it to Invincible, which is almost horror like in it's presentation. These Supernatural beings are capable of tearing mortals and one another apart quite easily.<br /> <br /> It's not terrible, but it certainly changes the way things feel if every fight that matters is a slug fest and there are 1 hit point &quot;Goons&quot; the PCs are supposed to be able to take in swarms.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think it depends a lot on how you imagine horror working, which gets to be a very complicated question when it's horror from the PoV of the monsters.<br /> <br /> I do generally think it matches my preferences. I don't think fights against significant enemies will turn into slugfests (although the quadratic effect of higher-tier enemies both having higher Defences and inflicting greater Complications might be an issue, although not one that *reduces* damage) so much as just be pretty guaranteed to not be over in a single round. Basically this and the strong reliance on Reaction Dread Powers (which I also have mixed feelings on) should eliminate situations where your big final villain either needs Fortitude and Celerity out the wazoo or else they're guaranteed to get one-round bodied without taking actions.<br /> <br /> Similarly I don't mind one-hit-point Mooks because I do think that in general the average supernatural should be able to violently dispatch the average mortal without much difficulty. I think I've joked in other threads about how common it was in Vampire (and it's a particularly noted issue with V5) for a hungry vampire to lose control and attack an innocent, only to lose the fight with the innocent because if you don't take the right skill and discipline combinations a vampire is just not very dangerous, especially if they're low on blood.<br /> <br /> Basically my preferred version of this kind of horror is one where regular mortals are extremely squishy (covered by 1HP shivers) but narratively significant characters don't just get wiped out by lucky dice rolls or bad initiative. So to me it works well.<br /> <br /> [Edit]<br /> <br /> I think the other thing I'd say is that I often find deadly combat an ironically bad fit for horror. To me the actual risk of game mechanical death is often less scary than it is frustrating. Obviously there <i>are</i> games where random, ignominious death are part of the horror atmosphere (especially ones that skew Lovecraftian) but I think to me I tend to find PC death in particular often leads to a bit of a &quot;welp, better write up a new character&quot; feeling which isn't super immersive.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28625,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28625-nobody-special","user":"Nobody Special","date":". 11-19-2024, 03:27 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28625-nobody-special","name":"Nobody Special","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"219"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-19T13:15:31-08:00","id":1546153,"title":"","content":"Oh incidentally I got the Injuries for higher level enemies wrong ; it's 7 for Terrors / Shattered Spaces, 10 for Nightmares (although armour adds to this).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28625-nobody-special","name":"Nobody Special","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"219"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-19T15:52:23-08:00","id":1546159,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nobody Special</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/curseborne/1545983-weapon-and-armor-tags?p=1546126#post1546126\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's interesting how different your interpretations are from mine. I don't think I'd put stunning on so many things for example, and I think I'd put Long Range on hunting rifles, especially if I was using Versatile for throwing weapons.<br /> <br /> I think it's also interesting that what tags &quot;mean&quot; depends a lot on what you use other tags to mean. For example if you give all firearms Penetrating, it follows that anti-balliatic armour needs Invulnerable.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is my first run with any Storypath system (I'm more familiar with Chronicles and Exalted, myself), so I can't say I'm surprised others might see the tags differently than I did! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> To go a little deeper into my thinking: following what you explained to Orphan81, I noticed the sharp limit on how much damage can be dealt in a single turn - and that <i>damage over time</i> status effects (Bleeding, Burning, Fast-Acting Toxin, etc.) are the easiest way to &quot;cheat&quot; the damage limit. From this, I immediately drew the conclusion that the system seems intended to play up status effects. Chronicles, by contrast, requires an exceptional success (5 hits!) on most rolls before you can apply a Tilt to the target unless you accept a penalty on the attack roll, and even that's not a hard-and-fast rule (as many spells, etc. say that the exceptional success gives you nothing but the extra damage). In short, combat Tilts are a comparative rarity in Chronicles' rocket-tag scenario, where the first blow probably triggered Beaten Down for any target susceptible to it, if it didn't fill the target's health track entirely (and almost every splat has one subgroup specialized to pull off exactly that - Rahu werewolves, Destroyer demons, etc.).<br /> <br /> Consequently, I felt that with what Storypath and Curseborne seemed to be trying to do with combat and the apparent importance it was placing on status effects, <i>Stunning</i> made the ideal mirror for bludgeons to <i>Wounding</i> for the blades.<br /> <br /> <i>Piercing</i> and <i>Impenetrable</i> were more about the historical ability of crossbows and early firearms to rip right through chain armors like they weren't even there - the whole motivating factor behind the development of plate armors in the first place (hence the term &quot;bulletproof&quot; and guns/crossbows all having the former tag, while only &quot;hard&quot; armors have the latter). The real problem with medieval plate and the reason it was phased out was entirely economic - plate armor was (and still is) expensive to the point of impractical, even for governments!<br /> <br /> Anyway, if you're feeling up to it, I certainly wouldn't mind hearing your own thoughts and perspectives!<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","name":"r0gue_rob0t","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-19T16:42:21-08:00","id":1546162,"title":"","content":"I agree there's more emphasis on status effects in SPU/CB than in Chronicles but I think from a pure personal perspective I'd want to limit the more fiddly status effects in combat just because I think it can slow things down. The DoT effects are all pretty much the same (one Injury per round) with minor differences that it's not the end of the world if you forget, whereas I'm extremely confident that I will forget the difference between &quot;Stunned&quot; and &quot;Dazed&quot; every single time.<br /> <br /> I think I'm also just a bit aesthetically bothered by stunning weapons because they feel a bit gamey to me. I've not seen how it works in Actual Play but I'd be very concerned that Stunning a strong enemy could be an extremely good tactic (Nightmares get a primary dice pool of 12, that's a bit over 4 successes on average, which means they're not *super* likely to be able to buy off that Moderate complication reliably, making them potentially quite stunlockable). It's the kind of effect I'd rather save for special attacks or magic.<br /> <br /> My general thought is actually that I approach from quite a different starting point to you; I don't think it's a good idea to come up with a single master weapons list (although your examples are a great jumping off point) and instead to use the tags relatively depending on the kind of game you're actually running and what kind of weapons would actually show up in it. It's a somewhat extreme example, but I'm planning a game set in the early 18th century, and so I don't really need to think about modern weapons or medieval weapons, I just need to make sure I've got rules for cutlasses and flintlocks.<br /> <br /> I'm in two minds on Impenetrable. Part of me thinks it's meant to be reserved for pure magic armour (although it usually says if that's the case) but I fully see the logic of using it to represent &quot;bullet proof&quot; armour in a world where it's assumed that most armour doesn't stop bullets (and is also assumed that bullets are, like, a thing so it's not a pre-gunpowder setting). I will also say that I'm not 100% sure what Defensive Advantage actually *does*, especially for NPCs. Combat is a bit weird because it's not *strictly* an opposed roll, you get Attacks against static difficulty but that difficulty can then be modified by Defensive Actions. In theory I think that would mean that Defensive advantage both increases the Difficulty to attack you by 1 *and* gives you a +2 Enhancement on Defensive Actions but then NPCs *don't* roll Defence, they just have a static value, and that number isn't modified by Impenetrable armour in stat blocks (whereas it *is* modified by Fitted armour).<br /> <br /> Finally, my instinct is to use Versatile the opposite way around from the way you use it, to have it represent pistol weapons that can be used in melee range. This is mostly because otherwise I think thrown weapons have *way* too long an effective range. &quot;Medium&quot; is really far in this game, I'd generally assume you can only throw to Short.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28625-nobody-special","name":"Nobody Special","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"219"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-19T18:25:11-08:00","id":1546172,"title":"","content":"All very good points, thanks! I'll certainly admit <i>Stunned</i> has the potential to be quite overwhelming, if someone can manage to apply it reliably. Perhaps too much so; yet I still like the idea of <i>something</i> to give bludgeons their own separate feel from blades... I might need to look back at the available options and think on it a bit...<br /> <br /> My read on Durability Advantage and antagonists - they don't include it in the antagonist's Defense score directly because it only applies if the opponent lacks the Power Advantage to counter it. This was part of why I was so restrictive with <i>Heavy Weapon</i> (along with it counting as two tags, unlike <i>Impenetrable</i>) - a venator surprising the Accursed in plate armor (whether old fashioned medieval or the modern ballistic jacket with ceramic inserts equivalent) honestly should be a serious &quot;Oh, shit!&quot; moment for the unprepared! (Same goes with Power Advantage for Accursed with no Durability Advantage stumbling blindly into the kill zone of a crew-served machine gun manned by venators. The kind of moment Flit is made for, really.)<br /> <br /> With thrown weapons, eh. The game probably says medium because reach weapons already go short. Drop reach and polearms lose a lot of significance. Change only the thrown weapons and you suddenly can't throw them past the reach of any old spear. Boost other range weapons to long by default and you need a 4th extreme range band to make the <i>Long Range</i> tag mean anything. Use the <i>Long Range</i> tag for any non-thrown ranged weapon that isn't a pistol and there's less room to differentiate all those weapons because tags are limited. It's enough of a corner case, yet one that is still likely to come up at some point, that your &quot;what kind of game you're running&quot; approach might be the only reasonable way to decide (even if I still feel it would be better to have more examples of the tags actually being used - especially in a mundane capacity - than currently exist in the book).<br /> <br /> Of course, part of that is just my previous experience with Exalted 3E/Essence, which has its own predetermined setting, absolutely uses the tags all in specific ways, and gives you a large predefined list of weapons both mundane and Artifact and how the tags apply to all of them. Curseborne absolutely does not have to be that, of course. But I do like the clarity of purpose that gave to each tag in Exalted. Different approaches, as you said!<br /> <br /> All great things for me to think about!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","name":"r0gue_rob0t","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-19T19:20:20-08:00","id":1546176,"title":"","content":"Giving some thought to the subject of <i>Stunning</i>: would you say that some of the weapons that have <i>Stunning</i>, but not <i>Brutal</i>, should have <i>Brutal</i> instead? Like the baton, staff, flail, knuckleduster, and/or boomerang? (I don't want to say morningstar because then it's just a clone of the longsword, and I feel like large shields make more sense disorienting people with a bash than doing massive damage.)<br /> <br /> Originally, I chose <i>Stunning</i> and <i>Wounding</i> to represent blunt and edged weapons, respectively, with Brutal representing the big, two-handed heavy hitters. But I can definitely see blunt weapons having easier access to <i>Brutal</i> as a way to keep <i>Stunning</i> a bit more exclusive in application, while still giving the bludgeons their own distinct character from the sharps.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","name":"r0gue_rob0t","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-20T05:15:27-08:00","id":1546188,"title":"","content":"So I'd personally keep it case by case with the choice based as much on what the individual player wants to emphasise as anything else. <br /> <br /> Wrecking is an option, or ironically you could go with piercing because historically blunts are actually better anti armour than sharps on account of how concussive force works. Although that seems against the intended flavour. <br /> <br /> I'd also make knuckledusters concealable.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28625-nobody-special","name":"Nobody Special","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"219"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-20T07:43:24-08:00","id":1546196,"title":"","content":"Oh very briefly, on thrown weapons (I didn't notice your earlier reply): I take your point that reach weapons cover Short range already but I think the difference is that throwing weapons don't as written need a tag (there's just a general rule that says Short Range is the range of thrown weapons, and when I say &quot;general rule&quot; I mean &quot;it's mentioned under range bands&quot;).<br /> <br /> As for Impenetrable - I think we have different ideas about how big a deal a Venator in Plate Armour should be. There's loads of ways to deal with plate (even more so modern ballistic plate which leaves large chunks of the body exposed) that feel like they should be especially open to supernatural beings.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28625-nobody-special","name":"Nobody Special","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"219"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-20T09:08:44-08:00","id":1546199,"title":"","content":"That's a cool list. I think I'd do things a little differently.<br /> <br /> As someone who has done a fair amount of both shooting and archery, that a hunting rifle should have long range and a long bow shouldn't. It's so so much easier hitting a target at a range with a rifle than with a bow. I think I would give more weapons Brutal and fewer weapons Piercing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1129-sergeant-brother","name":"Sergeant Brother","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1129_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-20T15:41:04-08:00","id":1546217,"title":"","content":"I'm honestly super-hesitant to put <i>Wrecking</i> on anything mundane. It would be a nice tag if it only affected objects, not armor... but as is, its effect on armor seems way too potent for me to feel comfortable putting it on anything that's supposed to be a mundane weapon. Some kind of potent acid or other corrosive substance, maybe. A transformed Hyde's fists, though? They're Accursed, they're allowed to just shred other people's armor like it's a single sheet of office paper.<br /> <br /> Thinking more about it, it may be a good idea to keep <i>Stunning</i> on batons (for blackjacks) at least. Maybe also boomerangs, since you aren't assumed to be carrying a whole dozen of them like with throwing knives (or give bolas their own separate profile, if I'm going to bother mentioning them - why don't we have any tags for tripping or grappling weapons?). Staves and flails/nunchaku could probably switch to <i>Brutal + Reach</i> or <i>Light Weapon</i>, respectively, while knuckledusters instead go <i>Concealable + Worn</i>.<br /> <br /> Hmm... So would you say <i>Long Range + Wounding</i> for the hunting rifle, Sergeant Brother? And <i>Wounding</i> with no other tag for the bow? I'd have to decouple the crossbow from the hunting rifle, since it shouldn't necessarily have the extended range, but should have <i>Piercing</i> (in context of interaction with the way I defined different armor types). Loss of <i>Piercing</i> in favor of <i>Wounding</i> on pure hunting weapons makes since, as they often use ammunition that is more likely to guarantee a kill but also more likely to be stopped by &quot;soft&quot; armor.<br /> <br /> I feel like I'm going to need to edit the posts with the list, soon - I'm really grateful for the feedback!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","name":"r0gue_rob0t","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-20T17:03:44-08:00","id":1546221,"title":"","content":"It gets tricky when we mix medieval and modern weapons. By medieval standards, a long bow is absolutely a long range weapon. In the Middle Ages, a crossbow is an armor piercing weapon. When you compare either to a modern rifle and neither is impressive.<br /> <br /> My first inclination is to make hunting rifles have Long Range and Brutal, but I can see expanding ammo having wounding instead. That opens up the possibility of different ammo types having different tags.<br /> <br /> I think that longbows should have wounding. In the modern era I don\u2019t know if I would give them anything else. In that case, wounding would represent the arrow stuck in your body tearing your flesh and wiggling around making you have to be careful or remove it.<br /> <br /> I could actually see giving some bludgeoning medieval weapons the Piercing tag. A blow with a mace or warhammer to the head may not actually make a hole in the helmet, but the impact could still concuss the victim. Should that mean stunning too? I\u2019m not sure.<br /> <br /> I could see breaking up pistols into heavy and light pistols. Light pistols are concealable and versatile. Heavy pistols are brutal and versatile.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1129-sergeant-brother","name":"Sergeant Brother","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1129_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-20T19:42:12-08:00","id":1546224,"title":"","content":"The way I see it, unless you're playing a &quot;Dark Eras&quot; type game, as long as melee exists we're stuck with a mix of medieval and modern kit regardless - even with everyone else wielding guns, there's always a chance <i>someone</i> is going to ask for that katana (never mind the boot knives). It's in that particular light that I chose to make <i>Piercing</i> so common with ranged weapons - I know there *is* a difference in the armor-penetrating capability of a crossbow versus modern armor-piercing bullets, but it's a distinction I would consider significantly smaller than the one between that crossbow and a purely muscle-powered bow or sword, and the way tags work doesn't exactly offer us different &quot;grades&quot; of penetrating ability, just a flat &quot;yes&quot; or &quot;no.&quot; Similarly, maybe kevlar vests and jackets could trade <i>Padded</i> for <i>Impenetrable</i> if modern soft armors are to behave significantly differently than medieval stuff, with the ablative plate inserts adding the <i>Padded</i> tag back on, but I personally wouldn't go that route - as with bow v. crossbow v. modern firearm, that kevlar vest's protective ability on its own is still closer to its fellow &quot;soft&quot; armors like medieval chain than to &quot;hard&quot; armors like those ceramic inserts or even just old medieval plate.<br /> <br /> This actually cuts to the heart of why I wish there had been at least a few examples of mundane gear. It's all well and good to make declarations at our own tables - that's <i>always</i> going to have to happen - but each of these tags exists for a purpose, to shape the events playing out at our tables in specific and intentional ways, and it wouldn't hurt to have a ready-made illustration of that purpose at hand beyond just a disconnected dots-on-a-character-sheet type rule when we encounter the need to make those calls. (As more than a few writers have said many times on these very forums, <i>fluff is crunch</i>, after all.) None of the tags exist in a vacuum, and there is no point assigning tags to weapons without also considering the armor side of things (and I mean all of the different armors, and also how it will line up against every other weapon as a result, and...). Sure, some of the tags seem obvious in their application (<i>Wounding</i>), but as seen with the different ways we've each applied <i>Versatile</i>...<br /> <br /> Speaking of, on the idea of using <i>Versatile</i> for pistol-whipping/butt-stroking, I can't say I'm on board with those kinds of attacks using the same set of tags that are applied when you pull the trigger and fire a bullet. That notion underpins my choice to use <i>Versatile</i> as the &quot;thrown weapon&quot; tag, where any other tag the weapon also possesses would absolutely be expected to still apply, in contrast with my idea of handling striking someone with the butt of a gun as if the character had used an entirely different weapon with fewer tags - or just no tags at all. Not saying you guys are somehow <i>wrong</i> to use it that way. I sure as hell can't claim to know exactly what each tag means more than anyone else outside the writing team. It's just that my read was that <i>Versatile</i> still applies all other tags, regardless of how the weapon is being used.<br /> <br /> But yes, I do like the idea of different ammunition changing up certain tags. I originally already included it for shotguns - though maybe slugs could stand to be different from buckshot, if I'm going to take this farther down that route!<br /> <br /> (Nobody Special, Sergeant Brother, I know this post might come off a bit more like I'm straight-up nitpicking some your ideas to death than my previous posts did - I'm sorry about that. I really do appreciate the back-and-forth, here, even when I find myself feeling, like now, as if I have to present a case for some of my own choices. Thank you, both of you. Seriously, you're both awesome. And ultimately, we all do still have the luxury if running it however we prefer at our own tables, and that will always be a good thing! For example: I do <i>like</i> the idea of having different sizes of pistols reflected, but I'm not sure if the tags make enough room for that while still preserving a meaningful distinction against the long guns. Though maybe light pistol = <i>Concealable</i> on its own, while heavy pistol = <i>Concealable + Piercing</i> or just <i>Piercing</i> on its own could work... though if the heavy pistol loses <i>Concealable</i>, the sawn-off will need to as well...)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","name":"r0gue_rob0t","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-20T20:56:47-08:00","id":1546227,"title":"","content":"I think that this is a good back and forth. To some degree, these weapon qualities are relative, so there needs to be some starting point on which to judge things. <br /> <br /> When it comes to making pistols versatile, I don\u2019t mean that you\u2019re pistol whipping your opponent. I mean you\u2019re shooting them at point blank range. Pistols are by their nature short ranged weapons, they are hand to hand weapons, a high percentage of people killed by handguns are within 10 feet of the shooter. If the pistol were out of bullets and you\u2019re hitting people with it, I would probably just consider that unarmed or a weapon with no tags.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1129-sergeant-brother","name":"Sergeant Brother","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1129_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-21T06:52:11-08:00","id":1546241,"title":"","content":"I spent the whole of October making comments to the devs like &quot;the spell list is inconsistent in its use of the word sentient which sometimes implies human level cognition and sometimes doesn't&quot; so I have zero problem with nitpicking.<br /> <br /> <br /> I agree re: Wrecking, full armour destruction is probably pretty extreme (although maybe not as extreme as it seems since many enemies won't have armour anyway and natural armour seems immune).<br /> <br /> For balancing medieval weapons vs modern; this might be a playstle experience thing but I don't think I've ever had a player force the issue of how effective medieval plate would be in a modern day game.<br /> <br /> This system seems positively designed to be used ad hoc, and it can only really work at all if you're either comfortable having a ton of weapons using the same game mechanics or you have a very limited weapons pool. The more axes of differentiation you try to encompass (modern vs ancient, light vs heavy, thrusting vs slashing vs blunt, pistols vs longarms vs sniper rifles vs thrown weapons) the more you'll run into hard constraints.<br /> <br /> To me the system is best suited for saying (in essence) what's important to *you* about *your* weapon. I'd even say it was valid for the same weapon to have different tags of used by different people.<br /> <br /> (Oh and for clarity, yes like Sgt Brother I wasn't imagining Versatile as pistol whipping, I was imagining it as close range shots)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28625,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28625-nobody-special","user":"Nobody Special","date":".  autocorrect 11-21-2024, 10:53 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28625-nobody-special","name":"Nobody Special","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"219"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-21T15:29:37-08:00","id":1546261,"title":"","content":"Wow... both of your comments re: <i>Versatile</i> had me running back to check the rules manuscript in case I missed something! NWoD/Chronicles <i>did</i> have a rule about ranged weapons in close combat, after all, complete with special rules for pistols, but it appears to me that Curseborne does not, instead just giving us &quot;Ranged Combat attacks are made at up to medium range with weapons such as...&quot; and moving on to tricks after the listed examples.<br /> <br /> I wouldn't blame any SG who said no rifles at close range, though, so I can see where both of you are coming from, here. (Heck, I've even messed around with other systems irrelevant to this discussion where you very explicitly <i>are</i> required to use the Close Combat equivalent to use pistols in melee.) It certainly doesn't help that the name of the tag is <i>Versatile</i>, not &quot;Thrown,&quot; &quot;Throwable,&quot; or &quot;Throwing,&quot; implying it <i>could</i> be used for skill substitution in either direction. I expected it to be used for Might + Close Combat -&gt; Might + Ranged Combat for throwing weapons, as hinted at in the description of the Ranged Combat skill, but is it also stated somewhere to be required for Dexterity + Ranged Combat -&gt; Dexterity + Close Combat in close quarters?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","name":"r0gue_rob0t","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-22T00:37:29-08:00","id":1546277,"title":"","content":"I agree that the default rolls for close combat might cut against the &quot;pistol&quot; interpretation of Versatile but that in turn might be a bit of a holdover from earlier versions of the system in which combat rolls had no default Attribute attached.<br /> <br /> Right now, as far as I can tell, it's not flat out stated that substituting Might + Close Combat --&gt; Might + Ranged Combat for a thrown weapon requires any kind of tag at all (and the range band descriptions softly imply that thrown weapons go to Short not Medium) so I think you're just sort of meant to eyeball it either way. Strictly there's no rule saying you can't fire a sniper rifle at somebody standing two feet away from you, or that you can't throw any weapon out to however far you like (except the Short Range range band description) so the Versatile tag arguably does nothing other than increase the range of a thrown weapon. Although it also doesn't *say* the weapon has to be thrown.<br /> <br /> A general observation I have about the system is that it's very mechanised in some places (like having lots of tags and keywords) and then completely unmechanised in others (like not really saying what weapons can be used in what contexts) which leads to peculiarities like this. I think it works fine in play if you're willing to spot rule a lot of stuff, and spot ruling a lot of stuff is clearly how it's *designed* to work, so in that sense it's perfectly valid as a design choice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28625-nobody-special","name":"Nobody Special","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"219"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-22T06:36:03-08:00","id":1546284,"title":"","content":"So if someone is using a rifle to shoot and opponent, and that opponent moves into hand to hand range, then what happens to the shooter? Can they keep shooting at the opponent who is in close combat with them? Obviously, it\u2019s possible to shoot someone under those conditions, but it does seem harder.<br /> <br /> If the rifle user can just shoot the enemy as normal, then there is no need to give handguns the versatile tag. <br /> <br /> I seem to recall that back in d20, trying to use a rifle in melee combat provoked an attack of opportunity, where as a hand gun did not.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1129-sergeant-brother","name":"Sergeant Brother","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1129_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-22T07:26:29-08:00","id":1546286,"title":"","content":"I just don't think the system models that kind of thing; movement in SPU is very abstract and there are get few hard and fast *rules* for anything. This is why I say it's not really the kind of game that's designed for a set equipment list; tags and status effects seem designed to be more narrative than simulational or tactical.<br /> <br /> in a white room situation, ranges other than &quot;long&quot; just don't matter because you can always attack anybody in medium range with any weapon. Outside of a white room situation, you're kind of expected to be making spot calls.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28625-nobody-special","name":"Nobody Special","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"219"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-22T16:05:03-08:00","id":1546294,"title":"","content":"Tags definitely <i>are</i> designed with narrativist approach in mind - Onyx Path typically approaches things from a perspective of &quot;crunch&quot; only mattering to the extent that it supports the intended &quot;fluff,&quot; which I like about their games - but it's the &quot;to the extent that it supports the intended 'fluff'&quot; part that has me grousing over this list while wishing we had a list of examples for how the tags are applied (to both mundane and magical gear, not just the magical stuff) the way Exalted gave us (both 3e and Essence). A mundane, random kitchen knife untouched by any curse, to use an intentionally extreme example, shouldn't be blowing past even the highest quality armors without a care, so it's still important for those of us using the system to be offered a concrete idea as to whether the <i>Piercing</i> tag means &quot;any thrusting weapon&quot; the way D&amp;D uses that word or &quot;a weapon specifically designed to penetrate armor&quot; the way Curseborne actually uses it. Unfortunately, not every tag is necessarily as obvious in application as <i>Piercing</i>. That's why I wish the book would have given us at least a few examples of items that would be common to almost everyone's games - at least enough to demonstrate the intended use of each tag. A set of reference points, to help new and experienced players alike navigate the application of these tags at their own tables.<br /> <br /> I cooked up this list partly as a study in how the tags interact with each other mechanistically and what that will mean for any emerging narrative attempting to use them. (Hence why I used the Blessed Plate's tags, stripped down to create a two-tag mundane suit of armor, as my starting point. The example relic weapons, sadly, have a variety of tags that all make sense for them individually as cursed items, but maybe not so much when trying to scale them back to mundanity.)<br /> <br /> <br /> Anyway, the changes I'm currently looking at:<br /> <br /> Remove <i>Stunning</i> from the staff, flail, and knuckleduster, replace with <i>Brutal</i> for the first two and <i>Concealable</i> for the last one. (Baton also? Or should it keep <i>Stunning</i> for the image of clubbing someone over the back of the head with a sap, crowbar, or nightstick and knocking them senseless? Maybe leave a note on it for anyone looking at the above list that you could take it either way, as long as it has only one of the two tags to differentiate these lighter bludgeons from the heavier maces?)<br /> <br /> Remove <i>Long Range</i> from the bows? (Another good spot for a note to the reader?)<br /> <br /> Combine the non-automatic rifles into a single rifle profile with <i>Long Range</i> and an ammunition-based variable tag (<i>Piercing</i> for armor-piercing rounds, <i>Wounding</i> for expanding rounds.)<br /> <br /> Slings and slingshots - just <i>Concealable</i> like blowguns? Separate and give slings <i>Brutal</i> and <i>Concealable</i> in line with the changes to close combat bludgeons, while slingshots only have <i>Concealable</i>?<br /> <br /> Split the pistol into light and heavy variants, with the light pistol having only <i>Concealable</i>, while the heavy pistol has that and an ammunition-based tag in line with rifles?<br /> <br /> Crossbows and premodern firearms get separated from modern pistols and rifles with their variable ammunition. Muskets and crossbows retain <i>Piercing</i> and <i>Wounding</i>, and premodern pistols and pistol crossbows retain <i>Concealable</i> and <i>Piercing</i>? Blunderbuss likewise set to <i>Brutal</i> and <i>Wounding</i>, pistol version to <i>Concealable</i> and <i>Wounding</i>?<br /> <br /> Shotguns have <i>Brutal</i> (or <i>Concealable</i> if sawn-off) and an ammunition-based tag. Birdshot is <i>Long Range</i>, buckshot <i>Wounding</i>, beanbag shells <i>Stunning</i>. Exotic shells may enable more exotic tags like <i>Flaming</i>, if characters can get their hands on them.<br /> <br /> Would everyone say that looks better? Any other suggestions?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","name":"r0gue_rob0t","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-23T06:36:54-08:00","id":1546301,"title":"","content":"Breaking this way down into parts, I think there's a bunch of levers to pull here.<br /> <br /> I think at the highest level, we need to remember that weapons have *up to* two tags, so I'd very much go with two tags for good weapons, one tag for mediocre ones, and none for outright bad ones (still better than punching because you get an Enhancement).<br /> <br /> I think the other tags can kind of be broken down into a few categories: damaging categories like Brutal, Wounding, and Piercing; what you might call &quot;form&quot; categories like concealable, light, and Long Range; and then the weird stuff.<br /> <br /> I think what you want here is:<br /> <br /> - Obsolete weapons just don't have tags. You could maybe give flintlocks &quot;concealable&quot; but they're actually really bulky and unwieldy. If you're using Versatile for pistols, I'd give them that.<br /> - Mid-tier weapons should probably get only their Form tags. Your knucledusters, your flick knives and the like. Concealable/worn and concealable/light for example are fine.<br /> - Ranged weapons probably get a Form tag (concealable and Long Range most likely) and leave the damaging tag for ammo.<br /> - Good melee weapons (yes that's a vague term) probably need a Form tag and a Damage tag, and generally I'd use Wounding for precision weapons (you don't get immediate stopping power but you might bleed out), Brutal for choppy or smashy weapons and Piercing only for things that explicitly defeat armour.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28625-nobody-special","name":"Nobody Special","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"219"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-23T09:55:44-08:00","id":1546308,"title":"","content":"&quot;Obsolete&quot; may not be the best word, here, and there is a definite reason I avoided the notion. Many pre-modern weapons do absolutely devastating things to the human body in comparison to a single bullet from modern military small arms (grenades are another matter, of course) - there are a lot of strict international treaties regarding what a military weapon can be manufactured to be capable of that absolutely did not exist before World War I. (For example: most real-life hunters use ammunition against animals that would absolutely be considered an instant, no-questions-asked, automatic conviction of war crime if used against a human target in a military context. Expanding bullets are a huge no no in war, for precisely the same reasons they're considered the most humane choice for a hunter: they do lots of internal damage and kill very quickly, leaving little to no chance for a medic to potentially rescue the wounded - or for an animal to limp off somewhere unseen only to suffer needlessly and die slowly.)<br /> <br /> ...it's just that modern military small arms put <i>that damn many</i> bullets in the air. The only thing actually &quot;obsolete&quot; about most pre-modern weapons is how slow their <i>rate/volume of fire</i> is by comparison - a detail that is somewhat less relevant in close quarters and isn't especially narratively useful to a game that wants werewolves tearing at things with claws and teeth while angels swing flamings swords around. Fundamentally, this is what I was thinking in choosing to restrict <i>Heavy Weapon</i> - and thus Power Advantage - to the full-sized machine gun. Curseborne already essentially gives everybody Chronicles' Quick Draw Merit for free, handling drawing or stowing weapons as reflexive actions, and I figure it reasonably follows that reloading is handled in like fashion - at least up until that moment where a Complication goes &quot;click.&quot; While they aren't necessarily <i>common</i> the way the rules handle fights, I feel that Complications are a better way to bring in the limitations of pre-modern gear than penalizing functional tags that have nothing to do with slow rate of fire just because the weapon's design is &quot;old.&quot;<br /> <br /> I'd also be careful about trying to split the tags themselves into formal groupings - you'll run into problems that way that will absolutely require frequent deviation from the formula you gave above. To use an example that comes immediately to mind, most &quot;primitive&quot; designs of one-handed swords would lose what you termed as the &quot;form&quot; tag (<i>Light Weapon</i>), not the &quot;damage&quot; tag (<i>Wounding</i>) - they're still vicious cutting edges, and quite large, but less finely balanced for ease of use. For the most part, I handled this from a mindset sitting between narrativist and simulationist - which two tags can I use to tell the story of this weapon best? No tags or just one tag were themselves an answer I tried to save for a specific purpose - mostly for things that are halfway to being improvised weapons (small knives and simple bludgeons) rather than being purpose-built to kill. (As for the blowgun, there's that obvious extra tag heavily associated with them that was deliberately left off on account of being applied through a certain set of substances that can also be applied temporarily to a large number of other weapons, too...)<br /> <br /> All that said, you do have a great point that in modern settings where Kevlar is a thing, flintlocks and other early firearms should probably be &quot;demoted&quot; and separated from the crossbows they otherwise share tags with by the loss of the <i>Piercing</i> tag. The loss of effectiveness against the class of armor they would and should defeat in a &quot;Dark Eras&quot; scenario (chain) where they still have that tag is certainly less important than the fact that they definitely shouldn't be effective against the modern equivalents of that same class of armor (ballistic fiber jackets and vests).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","name":"r0gue_rob0t","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-23T10:29:58-08:00","id":1546309,"title":"","content":"Fair point that &quot;obsolete&quot; is a tricky word to use, although I thing when it comes to premodern firearms there's a lot more to it that just rate of fire. There's also accuracy, tendency to misfire, and so on. Although you're probably right that a single musket ball with the right charge of powder might do more to an unarmoured person than a modern .23 bullet I don't think I'd give the musket ball any tags to actually *represent* that because all of the other drawbacks of the weapon are so limiting.<br /> <br /> I do agree that formal categories are a bad idea, I was mostly thinking less in terms of formal categorisation and more in terms of keeping an eye to what tags *actually do*. Brutal is a good example here. Sure from a certain perspective a musket is a more &quot;brutal&quot; weapon than a handgun but is it actually, broad strokes, easier to kill somebody with? It's fiddly to use, if it's not already loaded and primed it's basically useless, it's just not a good weapon by modern standards. The reason I wanted to separate out tags was that the &quot;damage&quot; tags are the ones that actually make the weapon more *effective* relative to other weapons, as opposed to the &quot;form&quot; tags, which are more about just making the weapon work the way it's meant to.<br /> <br /> Give a musket Brutal and it's one of the best ranged weapons to use in a street fight (unless you're strict about reloading) and that seems like an odd call to me. Although as you say it depends on whether you're trying to &quot;tell the story&quot; of the weapon, or to think about the consequences the tags will have on in-game use.<br /> <br /> I also think the &quot;Dark Eras&quot; problem is best solved by straight up changing out the tags of historical weapons in their appropriate era. That's part of the reason I used the term &quot;obsolete&quot;. I don't think a flintlock should have any tags at all in a modern day game because I just don't think it's a weapon that should be considered an effective choice relative to more modern options, but in a game set in its own era, I'd give it Versatile and Piercing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28625-nobody-special","name":"Nobody Special","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"219"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-23T11:37:42-08:00","id":1546311,"title":"","content":"I get you on the &quot;Dark Eras&quot; problem, I'm just saying that even then a lot of weapons probably don't need to change tags all <i>that</i> much. For example: let that flintlock pistol keep <i>Versatile</i> in modern times (or Concealable if you aren't ruling that close range requires the Close Combat skill only), and only drop the <i>Piercing</i> tag. Especially for that modern Dead who really wants to pull one out with Through Time, or get her hands on a cursed flintlock relic. (Heck, she can even have <i>Piercing</i>, if she's willing to accept that as the benefit of the weapon's curse.)<br /> <br /> But that Roman gladius over there? <i>Light Weapon</i> + <i>Wounding</i>, whether you're talking the time of Rome itself, the Age of Sail, or today. No need to change it (or many other weapons) up at all.<br /> <br /> (Also saves you time fussing over specific types of swords, just the general size categories of dagger, one-hand sword, and longsword.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","name":"r0gue_rob0t","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-23T15:20:01-08:00","id":1546324,"title":"","content":"Y\u2019all may disagree, but I would make Brutal the go to tag for most weapons that hack, slash, stab, tear, or penetrate - and I would make wounding and piercing more relatively rare in comparison.<br /> <br /> Why? For a couple of reasons. Brutal is very straightforward, it makes it easier to inflict more damage on an opponent. A weapon with a sharp cutting edge is more likely to inflict worse damage than one with it, a club will inflict less damage than if you turn the club into a mace with metal spikes.<br /> <br /> Piercing ignores most armor and there are few weapons that ignore most armor. Modern rifles, at least rounds with a full metal jacket, are a weapon that ignore most armor. When I was in BLET, at the range we shot soft kevlar body armor with rifles and the bullet passed through both sides like it was cloth. The same vest which stopped a 44 magnum round. That indicates to me that they should be piercing. To stop such a round, you need armor with ballistic plates, which means it\u2019s own tag for that armor.<br /> <br /> I would make Wounding a bit rarer, even though any sharp weapon could be potentially wounding, in part because of book keeping. Having a bunch of characters in a combat suffering from bleeding and taking wounds or suffering a disadvantage seems tedious to me. For ranger weapons, I would give Wounding to shotguns (that cause massive tissue damage) and arrows (which stick into the body and continue causing problems).<br /> <br /> So, assuming we don\u2019t need to give handguns the versatile tag, which is convenient, here is how I would handle some weapons:<br /> <br /> Pistol: concealable, brutal<br /> Shotgun: wounding, brutal<br /> Rifle (FMJ): long range, piercing<br /> Rifle (expanding round): brutal, long range<br /> Rapier (or small sword, epee du combat): light, brutal<br /> Sword (arming sword, saber, katana, etc.): Wounding, Brutal<br /> <br /> <br /> I\u2019d honestly like to give rifles a 3rd tag, but I\u2019m staying within the 2 tag limit.<br /> <br /> This isn\u2019t to say that this should be definitive, I like other people\u2019s suggestions too and think that they make sense, but I think this is how I might envision things.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1129,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1129-sergeant-brother","user":"Sergeant Brother","date":". 11-23-2024, 06:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1129-sergeant-brother","name":"Sergeant Brother","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1129_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-23T18:21:02-08:00","id":1546325,"title":"","content":"I actually 100% endorse &quot;most things are brutal&quot; because yeah I agree it's the tag with least bookkeeping and I *super* don't care if most weapons are mechanically identical.<br /> <br /> I do think it's a little counter to the spirit of the system in that SPU seems to freaking *love* status effects but in some ways a very detailed weapon system is at odds with the whole of the rest of the game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude0e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28625-nobody-special","name":"Nobody Special","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"219"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-23T22:39:11-08:00","id":1546329,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sergeant Brother</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/curseborne/1545983-weapon-and-armor-tags?p=1546324#post1546324\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When I was in BLET, at the range we shot soft kevlar body armor with rifles and the bullet passed through both sides like it was cloth. The same vest which stopped a 44 magnum round.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's actually an incredibly helpful tidbit. While I've at least worked with a few pistols and hunting rifles myself, I don't have quite that much experience - and none at all with military gear. To me, this suggests it should really only be rifles (or muskets in a pre-modern setting) and full-size crossbows that get <i>Piercing</i>, not pistols or pistol crossbows. (Needle-swords like the estoc too, as their whole design paradigm was the exploitation of joints and moving parts on armored targets.) Would that sound more in line with your experiences?<br /> <br /> As for &quot;most things are <i>Brutal</i>,&quot; can't say I'm much a fan of that idea myself, but this is definitely a spot where it doesn't hurt to say to each his own! For me, at least, the situational benefits and drawbacks of the different tags are part of the charm, and the extra bookkeeping is worth the variability of scenarios they create - I'd hate to get stuck with a system like combat in NWoD 1e again. Just with blades versus bludgeons, for example: <i>Brutal</i> makes purchasing Critical easier, which is great for emphasizing that a well-placed strike with a bludgeon absolutely does not care if you're wearing armor or not, while <i>Wounding</i> only costs 1 hit, not 2 like a <i>Brutal</i> Critical, but can't be applied if you haven't worked your way past the enemy's armor and dealt an injury to them underneath it yet.<br /> <br /> It wouldn't hurt to have two separate equipment lists floating around for people to use, though - one more bookkeeping-intense, and one less. At least it gives us one useful advantage from Curseborne's lack of example mundane equipment!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","name":"r0gue_rob0t","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-24T08:55:58-08:00","id":1546339,"title":"","content":"Just to throw a spanner in the works, the &quot;Wounding&quot; tag says &quot;Armour negates this effect&quot;. It doesn't say what that actually means. I see the logic of saying you can apply Wounding as long as you apply an Injury but a character whose armour boxes are all filled is still wearing armour (their armour tags still apply for example) so it isn't at all clear that the Wounding tag applies even then.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28625-nobody-special","name":"Nobody Special","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"219"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-24T15:12:39-08:00","id":1546356,"title":"","content":"An interesting spanner to be sure... but one which has me reevaluating the <i>Wrecking</i> and <i>Piercing</i> tags, more than the <i>Wounding</i> tag.<br /> <br /> The more that I look at it, the clearer it becomes that quite contrary to Chronicles, at least, mundane armor appears to be intentionally worth less than dog spit in Curseborne. Why would any sane, rational player waste 2 hits on a <i>Brutal</i> Critical that's just gonna get soaked by armor alongside the base injury, when Sunder (or Destroy Object for ranged weapons with <i>Wrecking</i>) also costs 2 hits... and shuts down <i>all</i> of the target's worn armor's traits leaving the target to count as if unarmored, no questions asked, for the remainder of the scene.<br /> <br /> Yeah, the base injury before you buy Sunder (or a <i>Wrecking</i>-empowered Destroy Object) still gets soaked by the armor before it goes offline, but... that was going to happen regardless.<br /> <br /> (Note that Sunder, the close combat Trick, doesn't even require <i>Wrecking</i> to do this. By default, Sunder, which is melee only, destroys gear, while Destroy Object, which is ranged only, destroys cover or unattended objects - what the <i>Wrecking</i> tag allows is for each Trick to accomplish what is normally restricted to the other. Looks like I should have put <i>Wrecking</i> on the axes, as it turns out...)<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, &quot;armor&quot; generated by Impenetrable Skin (or Swift Defense with its Entanglement 2 upgrade) cannot be removed by Sunder or Destroy Object, even with <i>Wrecking</i>, and continues to provide any side benefits (few as they are) even once the bonus injury boxes are filled... potentially making them a near-complete immunity button to the <i>Wounding</i> tag (outside of Red Death, which is Ethereal and therefore requires armor with Magic Resistance).<br /> <br /> Your thoughts here?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","name":"r0gue_rob0t","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-24T18:16:08-08:00","id":1546359,"title":"","content":"I *definitely* would want actual play experience of this before getting too deep in the weeds, you've pointed out yourself on another thread that getting two extra Hits is harder than it seems unless you have a ton of Enhancement.<br /> <br /> I do think that it might be closer than it seems in practice. Mundane armour has an absolute maximum of 3 Injuries (and only if double padded).<br /> <br /> Against an unarmoured opponent, Critical is better. Against +1 armour, Critical does more damage (since you get one damage soaked and one through) at the cost of letting the opponent continue to use armour tags, against +2 or +3 Sunder is better. <br /> <br /> *But* there's the PC to NPC thing to consider. It's only really Terrors and above her better than 1 armour and not all of them get it. Of those who have armour it's often natural so *probably* immune to Sundering even if not explicitly stated (I don't think you can Sunder the armour of a haunted house). So for PCs it will barely come up. <br /> <br /> Foe NPCs, this is my personal preference but I think NPCs should be run to create a good scene, not to optimise strategy so it won't matter if most of them don't &quot;think&quot; to Sunder off a PC's armour.<br /> <br /> It is interesting though. I kind of want to play with it to get a sense of how it works at a real table.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28625-nobody-special","name":"Nobody Special","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"219"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-24T20:46:14-08:00","id":1546365,"title":"","content":"I won't deny the fact that we've hit some &quot;white room&quot; stuff here (with all the attendant problems associated with that). The specifics of Critical versus Sunder end up being more about whether or not the other benefits of armor remain online after the bonus health boxes are exhausted. If yes, then you'll want Sunder even at +1 to get those pesky Impervious or Fitted tags out of the way ASAP; otherwise, it's six one way half a dozen the other at +2 and even at +3 only actually matters if the two extra hits needed for Sunder come up immediately on the first successful attack. But while those kinds of semantics might be relevant to a project like mine in trying to build a cohesive table of weapons and armor drafted to use the tags to play off of each other in narratively-appropriate ways, I won't deny it definitely misses the six million dollar question, one you've alluded to indirectly many times: Just exactly how many <i>significant</i> antagonists are actually going to <i>use</i> mundane gear?<br /> <br /> Even as much value as I see in drafting my list of mundane equipment, I know perfectly well what the answer to that question is: &quot;Very few.&quot;<br /> <br /> Venators, mostly - and venators aren't supposed to be <i>that</i> common. Social threats might surround themselves with leg breakers wielding this kind of stuff. Maybe the odd rival Accursed who is almost as green as the crew of Entanglement 1 PCs are themselves, or a Phantasm terrorizing people with telekinesis and whatever happens to be lying about.<br /> <br /> It has been a really good opportunity for me to think on how different parts of the system fit together as I draft my first characters in it, though! The map may not be the territory, but used properly it's still a tool to navigate with!<br /> <br /> (And yeah, pondering the rules like this is really making me want to get some friends together so I can see how it all plays out at a real table myself!)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","name":"r0gue_rob0t","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-04T21:55:16-08:00","id":1546626,"title":"","content":"I have now updated the lists in line with some of the feedback provided. Thanks again, Nobody Special and Sergeant Brother!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude0e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","name":"r0gue_rob0t","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}